page guide_apps {
  title = "Shared apps"
  chapter_header "Shared apps" {
    kicker       = "The guide · explanation"
    reading_time = "8 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-30"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<'DOC'
A shared app is one guest program whose windows appear on the host's own
desktop, beside the host's windows, with no VNC console in between.
`vmlab app run win01 -- notepad.exe` opens Notepad as a host window, and
closing it ends the session. This chapter explains which protocol carries the
windows, what each side needs installed, who the program runs as, and what
Windows does differently. The verb and its refusals are in
[vmlab app](cmd_app).
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
Sharing an app is something you do to a running machine from the command
line. The lab file declares nothing for it, and scripts cannot do it: they run
inside the lab daemon, which has no desktop to put a window on.
DOC

  h2 "Which protocol carries the windows"

  p <<'DOC'
vmlab has no window protocol of its own. Each guest family already has one
that sends individual windows to another machine: waypipe for Wayland
programs, X11 for X programs, and RDP RemoteApp for Windows. vmlab carries
that protocol's bytes over the agent channel. No guest NIC is involved, so a
shared app works on a machine with no network at all, and no port is opened
on the host or in the guest.
DOC

  table {
    rows:
      | "Guest" | "Host session" | "Protocol" | "Guest needs" | "Host needs" |
      | "Linux" | "Wayland" | "waypipe" | "`waypipe` on the login's `PATH`" | "`waypipe`" |
      | "Linux" | "Wayland, guest or host without waypipe" | "X11, through Xwayland" | "nothing" | "Xwayland (`DISPLAY` set)" |
      | "Linux" | "X11" | "X11" | "nothing" | "the X server" |
      | "Windows" | "Wayland or X11" | "RDP RemoteApp" | "an edition with an RDP host" | "FreeRDP 3 (`xfreerdp3` or `xfreerdp`)" |
  }

  p <<'DOC'
`vmlab app run` reads the host session from its own environment:
`WAYLAND_DISPLAY` set means Wayland, otherwise `DISPLAY` set means X11. That
is why the host end runs in the CLI process and not in a daemon. Only a
process started inside your desktop session can reach the compositor or the
X server, and the lab daemon stays headless. The daemon opens the agent
session and hands the CLI two private unix sockets. The CLI then starts the
host end of the protocol against them: `waypipe client`, a proxy to your X
server, or FreeRDP.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
The step from waypipe down to X11 is the only automatic one. It happens when
the guest has no `waypipe` for the login, or the host has none, and the host
has `DISPLAY` set. The command says it fell back, and why, on standard error.
`--via waypipe`, `--via x11` and `--via rdp` choose outright and never fall
back.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
Prefer waypipe on a Wayland host. X11 carries no compression and no shared
memory, so a busy program is slower under it, and a program with no X11
backend does not run under it at all.
DOC

  h2 "What to install"

  p <<'DOC'
On the host, install what the table names for the guests you use. vmlab finds
`waypipe` on `PATH`, and FreeRDP as `xfreerdp3`, then `xfreerdp`. Arch and
Debian ship FreeRDP 3 as `xfreerdp3`; Fedora ships it unsuffixed. To use a
binary somewhere else, set `waypipe` or `freerdp` in the
[host configuration file](ref_host). The CLI reads that file itself, so the
change applies to the next `vmlab app run` with no daemon restart.
DOC

  code wcl { filename = "~/.config/vmlab/config.wcl" source = <<'SRC'
import <vmlab-host.wcl>

host {
  waypipe = "/opt/waypipe/bin/waypipe"
  freerdp = "/usr/bin/xfreerdp3"
}
SRC
  }

  p <<'DOC'
In a Linux guest, install `waypipe` from the distribution so it is on the
login's `PATH`; X11 needs nothing in the guest. Both ends run with
`--compress lz4`, because a waypipe client and server that disagree on
compression refuse each other, so a guest waypipe built without lz4 cannot
serve a session. The agent starts waypipe through `/bin/sh`, which a minimal
container image must still have.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
In every guest, the agent must be one built with shared apps.
`vmlab machine capabilities <machine>` lists `app_waypipe` and `app_x11` for a
Linux agent and `app_rdp` for a Windows one. An older agent is refused by
name, and `vmlab machine repair-agent` replaces it in a running VM; see
[The machine's agent is out of date](apx_troubleshooting).
DOC

  h2 "Who the program runs as"

  p <<'DOC'
A shared app runs as a declared login, resolved exactly as for `exec` and
`shell`: `--user` and `--password`, else the machine's default login, else
the agent identity. [Logins](guide_logins) explains the ladder. The
waypipe check runs as the same identity, because the `PATH` that matters is
that login's.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
RDP cannot log on as the agent identity, which is SYSTEM. A Windows machine
with no `login {}` and no `--user`, or with `--user SYSTEM`, is refused with
`RDP needs an account`. Declare a login with its password, or pass `--user`
and `--password`.
DOC

  h2 "Windows: editions and the console session"

  p <<'DOC'
Windows Home has no RDP host, so it cannot share an app. The agent reads the
edition from the registry's `EditionID` and refuses a Home edition by name.
Pro, Enterprise, Education and Server editions work.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
Each RDP session starts with the agent preparing the guest. It turns Remote
Desktop on and allows RemoteApp to start programs that are not on a published
list (the `fAllowUnlistedRemotePrograms` policy and `TSAppAllowList`'s
`fDisabledAllowList`). It adds the account to Remote Desktop Users when it is
not an administrator, and starts TermService. It changes each setting only where it
differs, and leaves it changed afterwards. RDP traffic reaches the guest's
own `127.0.0.1:3389` from the agent, so no firewall rule is touched. FreeRDP
gets the login's credentials on its standard input, never on its command
line.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
The first shared app on a machine is slower to appear. Turning Remote Desktop
on starts TermService, whose listener takes about 40 seconds to accept a
connection. The agent waits for it, and `app run` prints
`[<machine>] waiting for Remote Desktop to listen on 127.0.0.1:3389` while it
does. FreeRDP starts once the listener is up.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
RemoteApp is a real remote logon, and that has a visible cost, because
Windows allows one session per account. A client edition always does, and a
Server edition does while "Restrict each user to a single session" is on,
which is its default. The RDP logon disconnects the same account's console
session, so `vmlab console` shows the lock screen while an app is shared. A
second `vmlab app run` against the same machine and account takes that
session over. The first app's windows move into the new FreeRDP process, the
old one exits, and the first command returns. vmlab states this rather than
working around it, because it is how Windows behaves. On a Server with that
policy turned off, each `app run` gets its own session.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
Closing FreeRDP, or pressing Ctrl-C on `app run`, disconnects the Windows
session rather than logging it off. The program keeps running in it, and its
windows come back with the next shared app for that account on that machine.
Close the program's own windows to end it.
DOC

  h2 "X11 and the host display"

  p <<'DOC'
For an X11 session the CLI mints a random cookie and the agent writes it into
a private Xauthority file for the program. The agent claims the first free
display from `:10` up. Every X connection the program makes reaches the CLI,
which checks the cookie and drops a connection that carries the wrong one. It
then swaps in your real display's cookie and connects to your X server,
the way OpenSSH forwards X11. A guest process that was not given the cookie
cannot reach your display.
DOC

  callout "An X11 app can see your other X windows" {
    kind = :warning
    body = "Once connected, the guest program has the same reach into the host X server as `ssh -X`, including reading input sent to other X clients. vmlab is not a security boundary between you and your guests, but this is one more reason to prefer waypipe on a Wayland host."
  }

  h2 "Worked examples"

  h3 "A Linux VM on a Wayland desktop"

  p <<'DOC'
Install waypipe and a program in the guest as root, then run the program as
the machine's default login. The window opens on your desktop and `app run`
returns when you close it, with the program's exit code.
DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab exec --user root web -- apt-get install -y waypipe gnome-text-editor
vmlab app run web -- gnome-text-editor
SRC
  }

  p <<'DOC'
On an X11 desktop, or with `--via x11`, the same command runs over X11 and
the guest needs no waypipe.
DOC

  h3 "A container"

  p <<'DOC'
A [lab container](guide_containers) shares apps like any Linux guest. Its
sockets live inside the container's root filesystem, where the program can
reach them. Add waypipe and the program to the image, or install them into
the running container:
DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab exec --user root dev01 -- apk add waypipe foot
vmlab app run dev01 -- foot
vmlab app run --detach dev01 -- foot
SRC
  }

  p <<'DOC'
The third line hands the session to a background helper and gives the
terminal back. The helper ends when `foot` exits.
DOC

  h3 "A Windows VM"

  p <<'DOC'
The machine needs an account with a password for RDP to log on as, so declare
one:
DOC

  code wcl { filename = "vmlab.wcl" source = <<'SRC'
vm "win01" {
  template = "x86_64/windows-server-2025"
  nic { segment = "lan" }

  login "dev" { user = "dev" password = "vmlab123!" default = true }
}
SRC
  }

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab app run win01 -- notepad.exe
vmlab app run --cwd 'C:\Users\dev' win01 -- notepad.exe notes.txt
SRC
  }

  p <<'DOC'
The provision that creates `dev` must run before the first shared app, as it
must before `vmlab exec` runs as that login. `app run` returns when FreeRDP
exits, with FreeRDP's exit code.
DOC

  h2 "What shared apps do not do"

  list {
    li "No wscript call shares an app. Scripts run in the headless lab daemon."
    li "vmlab turns on no audio, USB or printer redirection, although the protocols can carry some of them."
    li "vmlab writes no host launcher entries (`.desktop` files) for guest programs."
    li "macOS guests share no apps: `vmlab app run` refuses one by name, because macOS serves none of waypipe, X11 or RDP RemoteApp. Use `vmlab console` for its desktop. macOS hosts are not supported."
  }
}
